VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DEI-019
Reason for Visit: Acute panic attack, severe sleep disruption, and functional collapse triggered by the sudden cancellation of a long-awaited affirming surgical procedure.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and partner
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute episode of uncontrollable hyperventilation, severe despair, and a 48-hour cessation of sleep following administrative notification of care denial. Immediate out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was required to manage the acute psychiatric crisis, rule out acute cardiovascular stress response, and prevent potential self-harm prior to routine clinic hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: They cancelled my surgery after two years of waiting; I can't do this anymore, the system wants me dead.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a chronic history of overwhelming anticipatory anxiety regarding legislative bans and chronic bureaucratic fatigue from navigating exclusionary insurance policies. Yesterday, the patient was informed their gender-affirming surgery was indefinitely delayed due to administrative gatekeeping. This triggered an acute, severe mood destabilization characterized by uncontrollable panic, intense despair, and a total inability to sleep for 48 hours. The patient expresses deep feelings of systemic and institutional dehumanization, hypervigilance regarding the socio-political climate, and a disproportionate financial burden from out-of-pocket costs. The partner notes the patient has recently begun rationing existing hormone medications due to a pervasive fear of supply chain disruptions.
Functional Status: Acutely functionally impaired due to severe panic, lack of sleep, and overwhelming despair.
Social/Safety Concerns: Elevated risk of severe clinical depression and potential self-harm secondary to profound institutional betrayal and hopelessness.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 142/90 | HR: 105 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Patient appears exhausted, visibly shaking, tearful, and pacing the room with pressured speech.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Tachycardia, regular rhythm, normal heart sounds. Respiratory: Tachypneic, clear lung fields, no wheeze. Neurological: Alert and oriented. Cranial nerves intact. Fine tremor in bilateral upper extremities consistent with severe anxiety and sleep deprivation. The clinical interview is dominated by care access anxiety and a palpable sense of institutional dehumanization; the patient exhibits severe bureaucratic fatigue, struggling to recount the timeline of the surgery cancellation without breaking down.
Environmental Observations: Extensive files of medical paperwork, insurance denial letters, and appeal forms are scattered across the floor.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient is prescribed gender-affirming hormone therapy but admits to skipping doses over the past month to "stockpile" due to fear of losing access.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Gender-Affirming Care Access Distress - Adjustment Disorder (Secondary to Gender Dysphoria) - V65.49 (Unavailability of health care facilities) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of chest pain and symptoms directly correlating with the sudden, severe psychological trauma of the surgery cancellation.

Substance-Induced Anxiety Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely as the patient denies stimulant use, and the onset of the panic attack and insomnia was immediate and specifically linked to the administrative phone call.

Primary Bipolar Mania - Ruled out/unlikely as the pressured speech, pacing, and insomnia are purely reactive to a catastrophic loss of essential medical care, rather than a spontaneous, grandiose manic episode.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with an acute, severe stress response, including resting tachycardia (HR 105), elevated blood pressure (142/90), and profound insomnia, directly precipitated by the systemic trauma of denied gender-affirming care. Immediate out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was critical to exclude acute organic pathology from the severe autonomic arousal, force a break in the 48-hour insomnia cycle, and assess the immediate risk of self-harm driven by institutional despair. Delaying care until routine hours would have left the patient in an unmonitored state of severe psychological crisis, firmly satisfying the necessity for urgent MBS after-hours intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered a single dose of oral diazepam 5mg to rapidly down-regulate the sympathetic nervous system and induce sleep. Advised the usual GP to assess for elevated allostatic load biomarkers and psychometric indication of severe institutional betrayal, and to urgently intervene with the surgical clinic's administrative team.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage bureaucratic fatigue, implement a "containment strategy" for the administrative paperwork: physically place all insurance and medical documents into a single box and place it in a closet, committing to not opening it for 48 hours; removing the visual trigger will help decrease the chronic sympathetic drive. To address medication rationing, use a weekly pill organizer filled jointly with the partner every Sunday; delegating the visual check of the medication supply to a trusted partner removes the daily anxiety of scarcity and ensures consistent dosing.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to an LGBTQIA+ legal advocacy group and specialized psychological support.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP review within 48 hours to monitor mental state, review sleep quality, and coordinate an administrative advocacy plan.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or contact 000 if the feelings of despair transition into an active plan to end one's life, or if experiencing chest pain or shortness of breath at rest.

